    {
      "id": "decided",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 1260,
      "y": 292,
      "w": 604,
      "h": 135,
      "text": "• Godot, with the pinball physics as a C++ extension.\n• Each table is its own 2D physics plane, drawn in 3D.\n• Only playfields are simulated; ramps, rails and drops are scripted paths.\n• Builds for PC and the web (itch.io).",
      "size": 18,
      "color": "#1f1b16"
    },
    {
      "id": "fits-label",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 1240,
      "y": 455,
      "w": 624,
      "h": 30,
      "text": "How it fits",
      "size": 19,
      "weight": 700,
      "color": "#5b5f63",
      "caps": true
    },
    {
      "id": "fits-bar",
      "type": "rect",
      "name": "how it fits accent",
      "x": 1240,
      "y": 491,
      "w": 6,
      "h": 189,
      "fill": "#5b5f63"
    },
    {
      "id": "fits",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 1260,
      "y": 491,
      "w": 604,
      "h": 189,
      "text": "• Pillar 3, pure pinball: the playfield is real physics.\n• Camera: it follows the ball along a rail, then settles on the next table.\n• Business: a free portfolio piece, playable in the browser.\n• Risks: physics feel is the top risk, so it gets the C++ and the tuning.",
      "size": 18,
      "color": "#1f1b16"
    },
    {
      "id": "open-label",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 1240,
      "y": 708,
      "w": 624,
      "h": 30,
      "text": "Open",
      "size": 19,
      "weight": 700,
      "color": "#2f6fd6",
      "caps": true
    },
    {
      "id": "open-bar",
      "type": "rect",
      "name": "open accent",
      "x": 1240,
      "y": 744,
      "w": 6,
      "h": 81,
      "fill": "#2f6fd6"
    },
    {
      "id": "open",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 1260,
      "y": 744,
      "w": 604,
      "h": 81,
      "text": "• The web build needs the C++ extension compiled for the browser: prove it early in the prototype.\n• The physics step rate and how flippers feel at it.",
      "size": 18,
      "color": "#1f1b16"
    }
  ],
  "knowledge": [
    {
      "id": "k-engine",
      "kind": "decision",
      "text": "Godot, with the pinball physics as a C++ extension.",
      "why": "Godot for speed to a prototype; C++ where feel depends on it."
    },
    {
      "id": "k-physics",
      "kind": "decision",
      "text": "Each table is its own 2D physics plane drawn in 3D; only playfields are simulated."
    },
    {
      "id": "k-paths",
      "kind": "decision",
      "text": "Ramps, rails and drops between tables are scripted paths that hand the ball back at a set spot and speed.",
      "why": "A shot always comes out the same: tunable, and no 3D tunneling or jitter."
    },
    {
      "id": "k-builds",
      "kind": "decision",
      "text": "Builds for PC and the web (itch.io).",
      "why": "A portfolio visitor can play in one click."
    },
    {
      "id": "k-web",
      "kind": "question",
      "text": "Does the C++ extension build and run in the web export? Prove it early."
    },
    {
      "id": "k-step",
      "kind": "question",
      "text": "Physics step rate, and how the flippers feel at it."
    }
  ],
  "agent": {
    "brief": "Godot project; the ball and flipper physics live in a C++ GDExtension with a fixed time step. Each table is a separate 2D physics plane rendered in 3D. Anything off a playfield (ramps, rails, cave drops, return rails) is a scripted path with a fixed exit point and speed.",
    "rules": [
      "Never simulate ramps or rails: script them.",
      "Keep both PC and web builds working."
    ],
    "open": [
      "Web export with the C++ extension",
      "Physics step rate"
    ]
  }
